<p>Nathanael West&#8217;s 1939 novel <em>The Day of the Locust</em> contains a character named Homer Simpson:</p>
<blockquote><p>
Except for his hands, which belonged on a piece of monumental sculpture, and his small head, he was well proportioned. His muscles were large and round and he had a full, heavy chest. Yet there was something wrong. For all his size and shape, he looked neither strong nor fertile.
</p></blockquote>
<p>In a <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/arts-culture/matt-groening-reveals-the-location-of-the-real-springfield-60583379/">2012 interview</a> with <em>Smithsonian</em>, Matt Groening said, &#8220;I took that name from a minor character in the novel <em>The Day of the Locust</em>, by Nathanael West. Since Homer was my father’s name, and I thought Simpson was a funny name in that it had the word &#8216;simp&#8217; in it, which is short for &#8216;simpleton&#8217; &#8212; I just went with it.&#8221;</p>
